Software originally from:  https://gitlab.com/marinchip/

INTRODUCTION

These are the recreated source files for the MDEX operating system. The drivers
are surviving from the 1980's and the kernel source was mostly recreated in
the mid-80's by Peter Buro. The recommenting was completed in 2024.

MARINCHIP DISC EXECUTIVE - MDEX

As John Walker was bootstrapping his software stack, he needed a simple OS.
This became MDEX. It is similar to early CP/M or RT11 in its structure. In
order to be forward compatible, the system call interface was chosen to be
a subset of the NOS/MT operating system that John was already designing.
This proved very successful: nearly all programs that run under NOS/MT also
run under MDEX.

The unintended result was that MDEX became more popular than NOS/MT, also
because it did not need a hard disk (NOS/MT can run from floppies, but is
comfortable with a hard disk).

Being only 8KB in size with a simple, easy to understand internal structure,
it is a good match for retro 9900 based projects, as it gives such a project
an instant software stack to work with. It was the first OS to run on the
mini-Cortex.

John Walker writes on his website: "MDEX (which was originally called
Sisyphus Junior) was written with the Univac assembler, downloaded and
tested on my S-100 prototype machine. Debugging was via the PROM monitor,
which allowed examining and changing registers and memory without the
need for a front panel. MDEX, the Assembler, Linker, Editor, and Debugger,
were all originally written on the Univac assembler in its syntax.

The [Marinchip] assembler, however, used the standard TI syntax for its
instructions. When I wrote of MDEX being rewritten as 'native', I was referring
to converting the syntax of the assembly code from that used by the Univac
cross-assembler to native TI syntax which my assembler used and moving the
development from the Univac to the 9900 machine itself. By some time in 1979,
all development had moved to the 9900 and the Univac was no longer used.

I had access to the Univac because I was working for a company that owned it
and used it moonlighting. I had to move development to the 9900 because I
couldn't quit my job and lose access to the Univac until I did so.

In parallel with this software development, Fred Camerer, who had joined the
company as an equal partner, redesigned my two-board S-100 prototype into the
single-board design which became the M9900 CPU. This was designed by hand and
drawn as a logic diagram, from which the board was laid out using red and blue
tape at 4X scale. Part of the final layout was done on my dining room table.

Once we got and debugged the first run of five printed circuit boards, I
replaced the two board wire wrapped prototype in my IMSAI chassis with the
first M9900 CPU. It was completely compatible from the software standpoint, but
also had the ability to use 16-bit memory through the handshake protocol Fred
Camerer invented, which eventually became part of the S-100 standard
(IEEE-696-1983). It also brought out the 9900's CRU peripheral interface to
unused pins on the S-100 bus (of which there were plenty), which allowed access
to the serial ports on the PROM/RAM and eventual Quad SIO cards."

BUILDING

The MDEX kernel is a single 2400 line assembler file. Its main parts are
the command loop, the system call library and the console line editor -
complemented by a few libraries for message formatting, memory management
and floating point emulation.

It has three device drivers: one for the console terminal, one for the
printer and one for the disk drives.

The combined kernel is built with the make.sh script in the root directory
of this repo.

RUNNING

The Cortex emulator can run MDEX. It needs to be provided with two floppy
disk images, mdex1.dsk and mdex2.dsk respectively. These will be drive
"1/" and dirve "2/" inside MDEX.

Two images are provided: the MDEX release disk ("1/") and the basic games
disk ("2/"). Other images of original 640KB MDEX disks (for instance from
the www.powertrancortex.com website) can be used instead, such as the
QBasic, Pascal and SPL compiler disks.
